"""English — details, faith_b_2 (40 questions)."""

D = {
"faith_111": "Surah At-Tahrim calls: \"O you who have believed, repent to Allah with sincere repentance, so that your Lord may remove from you your misdeeds and admit you into gardens beneath which rivers flow\" (66:8). The same verse names the Day when Allah will not disgrace the Prophet ﷺ and those who believed with him. It follows the command to protect oneself and one's family from the Fire (66:6). A Muslim learns that repentance is a whole turning back, and that its fruit is forgiveness and light.",
"faith_112": "Surah Ash-Shura says: \"And it is He who accepts repentance from His servants and pardons misdeeds, and He knows what you do\" (42:25). The next verse adds that He answers those who believe and do righteous deeds and increases them from His bounty (42:26). Acceptance, pardon and full knowledge are joined in one sentence: the One who forgives is the One who sees everything. A Muslim learns that nothing about him is hidden from Allah, and that this is why he turns back to Him with hope.",
"faith_113": "The verse continues: \"and who can forgive sins except Allah? And they do not persist in what they have done while they know\" (3:135). The verse before describes the God-fearing who spend in ease and hardship, restrain their anger and pardon people (3:134); the verse after names the reward, forgiveness and gardens beneath which rivers flow (3:136). The praised people are not those who never slip, but those who, having slipped, remember Allah at once. A Muslim learns that turning back quickly is itself a mark of the righteous.",
"faith_114": "The two verses read: \"O you who have believed, remember Allah with much remembrance, and exalt Him morning and evening\" (33:41-42). The next verse gives the reason: \"It is He who sends blessings upon you, and His angels, that He may bring you out from darkness into the light\" (33:43). Earlier, the men and women who remember Allah much are promised forgiveness and a great reward (33:35). A Muslim keeps Allah on his tongue and in his heart, and marks both ends of the day with glorification.",
"faith_115": "The passage opens: \"Indeed, in the creation of the heavens and the earth and the alternation of the night and the day are signs for those of understanding\" (3:190). Their reflection ends in prayer: \"Our Lord, You did not create this aimlessly; exalted are You; so protect us from the punishment of the Fire\" (3:191). Their Lord answers that none of their work will be lost (3:195). A Muslim learns to see the sky and the turning of day and night as a call to remember Allah.",
"faith_116": "The command is addressed to the Prophet ﷺ: \"So by mercy from Allah you were lenient with them; had you been harsh and hard of heart, they would have left you. So pardon them, ask forgiveness for them and consult them in the matter\" (3:159). The next verse completes it: \"If Allah helps you, none can overcome you; and if He forsakes you, who can help you after Him?\" (3:160). A Muslim learns the order: gentleness, consultation, a firm decision, then trust in Allah rather than in his own plan.",
"faith_117": "Surah Ash-Sharh is addressed to the Prophet ﷺ and opens with a question: \"Did We not expand for you your breast, and remove from you your burden which weighed upon your back, and raise high for you your renown?\" (94:1-4). Then comes the promise, said twice: \"Indeed, with hardship comes ease\" (94:5-6). The surah closes by telling him to stand up when finished and turn his longing to his Lord (94:7-8). A Muslim learns that one task done is the doorway to the next act of devotion.",
"faith_118": "The verse names five things the worldly life is made of: \"amusement and diversion and adornment and boasting among yourselves and rivalry in wealth and children\" (57:20). Then it sets the Hereafter beside it, \"severe punishment, and forgiveness from Allah and approval,\" and ends: \"and what is the worldly life except the enjoyment of delusion\" (57:20). The next verse says instead: race toward forgiveness and a Garden as wide as the heavens and the earth (57:21). A Muslim learns to use this world without being deceived by it.",
"faith_119": "The verse ends with a comparison: the enduring good deeds are \"better with your Lord for reward and better for hope\" (18:46). It follows a parable: \"the life of this world is like rain We send down from the sky; the vegetation of the earth mingles with it, then it becomes dry stubble scattered by the winds\" (18:45). Before that, Surah Al-Kahf tells of the man with two gardens who boasted of his wealth (18:34) and lost everything. A Muslim learns to value what stays over what fades.",
"faith_120": "The question is asked near the end of Surah Al-Mu'minun: \"Then did you think that We created you aimlessly and that to Us you would not be returned?\" (23:115). Before it, those who wasted their lives are asked how long they stayed on earth, and answer, \"a day or part of a day\" (23:113). Then comes the answer: \"So exalted is Allah, the Sovereign, the Truth; there is no deity except Him\" (23:116). A Muslim learns that a purposeful life expects to return to Allah.",
"faith_121": "It reads: \"So remember Me; I will remember you. And be grateful to Me and do not be ungrateful\" (2:152). It follows the change of the qiblah and the favour of a Messenger sent from among the believers, \"reciting to you Our verses, purifying you, and teaching you the Book and wisdom\" (2:151). The next verse tells them to seek help through patience and prayer (2:153). A Muslim learns that his remembrance of Allah is met by Allah's remembrance of him, and that thanks is the reply to guidance.",
"faith_122": "The verse opens: \"O you who have believed, obey Allah and obey the Messenger and those in authority among you\" (4:59). Then comes the rule for disagreement, referring it to Allah and the Messenger ﷺ, and the verse ends: \"That is best and finest in outcome\" (4:59). The verse before it commands rendering trusts to their owners and judging between people with justice (4:58). A Muslim learns that in a dispute, the Qur'an and the guidance of the Prophet ﷺ are where he looks for the answer.",
"faith_123": "The verse continues: \"and upon their Lord they rely\" (8:2). The next verses add: \"who establish prayer and spend from what We have provided them; those are the believers in truth; for them are degrees with their Lord, and forgiveness, and noble provision\" (8:3-4). The surah opens with the question about the spoils of war and the command to set right what is between the believers (8:1), so this portrait answers it. A Muslim learns that faith is felt in the heart and seen in trust, prayer and giving.",
"faith_124": "The Prophet ﷺ is commanded to declare it, and it continues: \"No partner has He; and this I have been commanded, and I am the first of the Muslims\" (6:162-163). Just before, he is told to say that his Lord guided him to a straight path, \"the religion of Ibrahim, inclining toward truth\" (6:161). The verse gathers prayer, sacrifice, living and dying into one direction. A Muslim learns to intend his whole life, not only his prayers, for the Lord of the worlds.",
"faith_125": "The verse continues: \"inclining to truth, and to establish prayer and give zakat; and that is the correct religion\" (98:5). The surah speaks of the People of the Scripture, who divided only after clear evidence came to them (98:4), a Messenger reciting purified pages (98:2). They had been commanded nothing else: sincere worship, prayer and zakat. The surah then calls those who believe and do righteous deeds \"the best of creatures\" (98:7). A Muslim learns that worship counts when it is meant purely for Allah.",
"faith_126": "Surah Muhammad describes the garden: \"in it are rivers of water unaltered, rivers of milk whose taste never changes, rivers of wine delicious to those who drink, and rivers of purified honey\" (47:15). The same verse adds every kind of fruit and forgiveness from their Lord, then asks whether they are like those in the Fire who drink scalding water (47:15). Four rivers are named, each one unlike anything the earth offers. A Muslim learns that the Garden is described in things he can picture.",
"faith_127": "The verse belongs to a portrait of the believers in Surah As-Sajdah. They fall down in prostration when reminded of the verses (32:15); \"their sides part from their beds, they call upon their Lord in fear and hope, and from what We have provided them they spend\" (32:16). Then comes the hidden reward: \"No soul knows what has been hidden for them of comfort for the eyes\" (32:17). A Muslim learns that the night prayers no one sees are answered with a reward no one can imagine.",
"faith_128": "In full: \"For those who have done good is the best reward and more; no darkness will cover their faces, nor humiliation. Those are the companions of Paradise; they will abide in it forever\" (10:26). Before it is an invitation: \"And Allah invites to the Home of Peace and guides whom He wills to a straight path\" (10:25). The verse after sets the contrast: an evil deed is repaid only with its like (10:27). A Muslim learns that Allah's reward for good always exceeds the deed.",
"faith_129": "The verse reads: \"Those who feared their Lord will be driven to Paradise in groups until, when they reach it and its gates are opened, its keepers say to them: Peace be upon you; you have become pure, so enter it to abide eternally\" (39:73). They answer: \"Praise be to Allah, who has fulfilled His promise to us\" (39:74). Two verses earlier, another group is driven to Hell (39:71). A Muslim learns that the greeting of peace he gives on earth awaits him at the gate of the Garden.",
"faith_130": "The call in 3:133 is to hasten to two things: forgiveness from the Lord, and a Garden as wide as the heavens and the earth. The next verses say who is meant: \"who spend in ease and in hardship, who restrain anger and pardon people; and Allah loves the doers of good\" (3:134); and who ask forgiveness when they wrong themselves (3:135). Their reward is forgiveness and gardens beneath which rivers flow (3:136). A Muslim learns that hastening to forgiveness means giving, holding back anger and pardoning.",
"faith_131": "The verse goes on to describe that Fire: \"over it are angels, stern and severe, who do not disobey Allah in what He commands them, but do what they are commanded\" (66:6). Near its end it gives the wife of Nuh and the wife of Lut, who betrayed two righteous servants of Allah, and the wife of Fir'awn, who prayed for a house near Allah (66:10-11). A Muslim learns that guarding himself is not enough: his family is a trust he must also keep from the Fire.",
"faith_132": "After belief the verse turns to giving: wealth given \"despite love for it, to relatives, orphans, the needy, the traveller, those who ask, and for freeing slaves\" (2:177). Then come establishing prayer, giving zakat, keeping promises, and patience in hardship and battle (2:177). It ends: \"Those are the ones who have been true, and those are the righteous\" (2:177). It follows the verses on the direction of prayer, and says what facing it is for. A Muslim learns that righteousness is faith joined to open hands and a patient heart.",
"faith_133": "The verse continues: \"and you will be returned to the Knower of the unseen and the witnessed, and He will inform you of what you used to do\" (9:105). It follows the verses about those who confessed their sins, having mixed a good deed with a bad one (9:102), and the charity taken to purify them (9:103). Deeds are not secret: Allah, His Messenger ﷺ and the believers see them, and each is brought back. A Muslim works well, since his work is seen and will be shown to him.",
"faith_134": "The surah has four verses: \"Say: He is Allah, One; Allah, the Eternal Refuge; He neither begets nor is born; and there is none comparable to Him\" (112:1-4). It opens with \"Say\", so the Prophet ﷺ is told to announce these words, and moves from oneness to the denial of any child, parent or equal. The final verse (112:4) closes every door through which likeness could enter. A Muslim learns to think of Allah as unlike anything he knows, and recites this surah as the plainest statement of belief.",
"faith_135": "The verse reads: \"He is the First and the Last, the Manifest and the Hidden, and He is Knowing of all things\" (57:3). It opens Surah Al-Hadid, after the statement that all in the heavens and the earth glorifies Allah (57:1) and that His is the dominion (57:2). The next verse adds that He knows what enters the earth and what comes out of it, and \"He is with you wherever you are\" (57:4). A Muslim learns that Allah is before every beginning and after every end.",
"faith_136": "The names continue in the same verse: \"the Exalted in Might, the Compeller, the Supreme; exalted is Allah above whatever they associate with Him\" (59:23). The verse before opened the list by denying any other deity and naming Him Knower of the unseen and the witnessed (59:22); the verse after adds the Creator, the Originator, the Fashioner, \"to Him belong the best names\" (59:24). Three verses gather more than a dozen names. A Muslim learns these names as a way of knowing Allah.",
"faith_137": "The word shahadah means testimony: the one who says it bears witness to two things, that there is no deity but Allah and that Muhammad ﷺ is His Messenger. Both halves are found in the Qur'an, which says, \"Know that there is no deity except Allah\" (47:19) and \"Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah\" (48:29). A reader finds it in the adhan of every mosque and the tashahhud of every prayer. A Muslim learns that Islam begins with this testimony and that everything else he does rests upon it.",
"faith_138": "Takbir means \"making great\": to say Allahu akbar is to declare that Allah is greater than everything. The Qur'an commands it with the same root, \"and magnify Him with great magnification\" (17:111), and says the days of Ramadan are completed \"so that you may magnify Allah for having guided you\" (2:185). A reader hears it opening the call to prayer and every prayer, and at each movement within it. A Muslim learns that takbir, tasbih, tahmid and tahlil are the plain words of his remembrance.",
"faith_139": "The name basmalah comes from the formula's first words, bismi-llah, \"in the name of Allah\". A reader finds it at the head of every surah except the ninth, At-Tawbah, and once inside a surah, in the letter Sulayman sent to the queen of Saba: \"It is from Sulayman, and it is: In the name of Allah, the Entirely Merciful, the Especially Merciful\" (27:30). The formula names Allah by two names of mercy before anything else is said. A Muslim opens his recitation, his meal and his work with it.",
"faith_140": "The words, inna lillahi wa inna ilayhi raji'un, are given in 2:156 as the mark of the patient. It sits inside a promise of trial: \"We will surely test you with something of fear and hunger and loss of wealth, lives and fruits; but give good tidings to the patient\" (2:155). The reward follows: blessings from their Lord, mercy, and guidance (2:157). The sentence makes two statements: that we are Allah's, and that we go back to Him. A Muslim learns to answer loss with these words.",
"faith_141": "The instruction continues: \"And remember your Lord when you forget, and say: It may be that my Lord will guide me to something nearer than this to right guidance\" (18:23-24). It follows the sleepers of the cave, whose number people disputed; the Prophet ﷺ was told to say, \"My Lord knows best their number\" (18:22). The small word for tomorrow carries the same lesson: what is to come is known to Allah alone. A Muslim learns to say in sha' Allah when he speaks of the future.",
"faith_142": "The verse is the last of Surah Luqman, and it ends: \"Indeed, Allah is Knowing, Acquainted\" (31:34). The verse before warns of a Day when no father will avail his son, and tells people not to let this world deceive them (31:33). Five things are named in 31:34 that no creature can know in advance: the Hour, the rain, the wombs, tomorrow's earnings and the place of death. A Muslim learns to leave the unseen to Allah and to prepare for a Day whose time only He knows.",
"faith_143": "The verse reads: \"For every nation there is a term; so when their term has come, they will not remain behind an hour, nor will they go ahead\" (7:34). It follows the verse forbidding immoralities, sin, oppression and speaking about Allah without knowledge (7:33), and leads into the promise that whoever fears Allah and does right will have no fear nor grief (7:35). Surah Yunus repeats the sentence (10:49). A Muslim learns that every people's end is fixed, and the time before it is the time to reform.",
"faith_144": "The verse continues: \"and We will surely give them their reward according to the best of what they used to do\" (16:97). The verse before explains: \"Whatever you have will run out, but what is with Allah is lasting\" (16:96). The verse after tells the reciter of the Qur'an to seek refuge in Allah from Shaytan (16:98). Two rewards are promised to men and women alike: a good life here, and a reward measured by their best deeds. A Muslim learns that faith with good work is repaid twice.",
"faith_145": "The verse continues: \"And if you obey Allah and His Messenger, He will not deprive you of anything of your deeds\" (49:14). The next verse defines the believers: those who believe in Allah and His Messenger, do not doubt, and strive with their wealth and lives (49:15). The bedouins are told not to count their Islam as a favour to the Prophet ﷺ; Allah favoured them by guiding them (49:17). A Muslim learns that saying the words is a beginning, and that faith settles in the heart through obedience.",
"faith_146": "The rule follows a killing: the surah has just told of the two sons of Adam, one of whom, his offering unaccepted, killed his brother and was left regretful (5:27-31). The verse opens, \"Because of that, We decreed upon the Children of Israel\" (5:32), and lays down that one life killed weighs like all mankind and one life saved weighs the same. It closes: messengers came with clear proofs, and many still transgressed (5:32). A Muslim learns to see every single human life as carrying the weight of all humanity.",
"faith_147": "The verse ends: \"and they will not be wronged\" (6:160). It closes Surah Al-An'am, after the warning that the Prophet ﷺ has nothing to do with those who split their religion into sects (6:159), and before his declaration that his prayer, living and dying are for Allah (6:162). Surah Al-Baqarah likens spending in Allah's way to a grain that grows seven ears with a hundred grains in each (2:261). A Muslim learns that good is counted generously and evil only by its measure, so no good is small.",
"faith_148": "The verse reads: \"O mankind, you are the ones in need of Allah, while Allah is the Free of need, the Praiseworthy\" (35:15). The next verses add: \"If He wills, He can remove you and bring a new creation\" (35:16). Just before, the surah says the false gods own not even the membrane of a date-stone (35:13) and do not hear when called (35:14). A Muslim learns that his worship adds nothing to Allah and everything to himself, so he asks from the One who lacks nothing.",
"faith_149": "Surah Al-'Asr is three verses: \"By time, indeed mankind is in loss, except those who believe and do righteous deeds and enjoin one another to the truth and enjoin one another to patience\" (103:1-3). It opens with an oath by time, states the rule for everyone, then gives the one exception. Surah Al-Balad names the same pairing: \"those who believed and enjoined one another to patience and enjoined one another to compassion\" (90:17). A Muslim learns that faith and good deeds are kept by people who remind each other.",
"faith_150": "It goes on: \"We make no distinction between any of His messengers. And they say: We hear and we obey; Your forgiveness, our Lord, and to You is the return\" (2:285). It is the first of the two verses closing Surah Al-Baqarah; the second teaches the prayer, \"Our Lord, do not take us to task if we forget or err\" (2:286). Four objects of belief are named, and the messengers are accepted whole. A Muslim learns to make these words his own: to hear, obey and ask forgiveness.",
}
